From 979b847da9afc17e3c4c486d9261a1bb024b7234 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 21:51:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 04/34] Balance pipeline stages by peak memory, not layer count

Under 1F1B stage s keeps (pp_size - s) micro-batches live, so its footprint
is layers * (weights_per_layer + acts_per_layer * in_flight). Minimising the
largest stage ignores the in-flight term and loads the early stages hardest,
which is where the host mirror hits its ceiling first: x16 measured 488 GB
shared on stage 0 against 112 GB on stage 7, a 6.7x spread that killed the
early nodes while the late ones idled.

Weighting by activations alone is worse, not better -- it hands the last
stage 32 of 78 layers, and at ~1.5 GB/layer of weights that needs 52 GB on a
48 GB card. Both terms must be in the cost.

  before  layers [8,10,10,10,10,10,10,10]  peak 24.6 GB/GPU
  after   layers [8,8,8,10,10,10,12,12]    peak 21.9 GB/GPU

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_017wiESxhziDrnsLnG9aizTd
